# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DENY-LIST — and it has to be, unlike shipo-sales-dashboard.
#
# That repo is a static site, so it uses deny-by-default (`*` plus a short
# allow-list): every file served is a file someone chose to serve. This repo is
# a Next.js app that Vercel BUILDS from source, so `*` would starve the build.
# Here we ship the source and name the things that must not travel with it.
#
# Why this file exists at all: `vercel --prod` uploads the working directory,
# not the git index, so untracked files ship too.
#
# The one that actually matters is .env.local. Next.js loads it during the
# build, so if it rides along it can override the environment variables set in
# the Vercel dashboard — silently swapping production config for whatever is on
# this laptop. Vercel does not exclude it for you.
#
# Uploaded source is a build input, not a public URL, so this is a smaller blast
# radius than the dashboard repo's allow-list. It is not zero.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- secrets: never upload. Env vars belong in the Vercel dashboard. ---
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# --- local-only tooling, never imported by the app ---
scripts/
supabase/
docs/

# --- agent / editor / VCS scratch ---
.claude/
.git/
.vscode/
.DS_Store

# --- build + dependency output, rebuilt on Vercel ---
node_modules/
.next/
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
